module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Generate

let content () : String =
  """# Generators

A generator is a fn that writes Dark. It returns `Generate.Run`, a module's worth
of source text, and `dark generate` stages that source the way `module` stages a
file you typed: parsed, resolved, hashed, type-checked at rest, propagated. What a
generator makes is a type or a fn like any other. Nothing is "generated" as a kind.

  generate <libraryFn> --<param> <value>... --into Owner.Module   write a saved generator, run it
  generate <savedGenerator>                             re-run one
  ... --fetch <url>                                     take the document from a URL, not a file
  generate <savedGenerator> --refresh                   re-fetch its URL; re-run only if it moved
  ... --out <dir>                                       where a generator's files go (a .d.ts)
  ... --dry-run                                         show what would be written, stage nothing
  ... --print                                           print the source a saved generator produces

Library generators, by what they read:

  From a file (stored once as a blob, named by hash in the saved fn):
  Json.fromSample --name Order --sample order.json        records that parse the sample,
                                                          with decoders, encoders, `parse`
  Json.fromSamples --name Order --samples dir/            the same over every file in a
                                                          directory, shapes unified
  JsonSchema.types --name Ticket --schema schema.json     records for the root and $defs
  Csv.fromSample --name Person --sample rows.csv          a row type from the header and
                                                          columns, `fromFields`, `parse`
  Sql.schemaTypes --dump schema.sql                       a record per CREATE TABLE
                                                          (sqlite3 .schema), `<x>FromRow`,
                                                          `all<Table>`
  OpenApi.client --spec openapi.json                      a typed HTTP client (or
                                                          --fetch <url> instead of --spec)
  OpenApi.server --spec openapi.json                      `Handlers`, `notImplemented`,
                                                          `router` for `Stdlib.HttpServer`
  GraphQL.client --schema schema.graphql                  records, one fn per root field

  From a package type (a type argument, so the type is a dependency and edits
  regenerate):
  Dark.mirror --type Acme.Core.Money                      the same declaration elsewhere
  Derive.forType --type Acme.Core.Money                   show<T>, equals<T>, with<Field>
  Fixtures.forType --type Acme.Core.Order                 example<T> (), transitively

  From a module (by path; re-run by name):
  Dark.mirrorModule --source Acme.Core                    every item copied, references
                                                          rewritten to the copies
  TypeScript.toTypeScript --source Acme.Core --out ts/    a .d.ts file

Every argument is named after the library fn's parameter (`Json.fromSample (name:
String) (sample: Blob)` takes `--name` and `--sample`); a type parameter is `--type`.
A missing one is named back with what it takes. Your own generator gets the same
treatment: its parameter names are its flags.

Generated records read and write JSON as other systems do: a missing or null key is
None, and None is left out. That is `decode<T>` and `encode<T>` over
`Stdlib.AltJson.Decode` / `Encode`; `Stdlib.Json.parse` reads Dark's own encoding of
Option and is not what a generated `parse` uses.

Short names resolve: `Json.fromSample` finds `Darklang.Generate.Json.fromSample`.

## The saved generator

The library form is sugar. It writes ONE fn into the target module, whose body is
the call you made, and runs it:

  $ dark generate Json.fromSample --name Order --sample ./order.json --into Acme.Orders
    stored ./order.json as blob 7f3a1c9e (412 B)
    wrote Acme.Orders.orderGenerator
    ran Acme.Orders.orderGenerator into Acme.Orders:
      + Order
      + OrderLine
      + parse

  $ dark view Acme.Orders.orderGenerator
    let orderGenerator () : Darklang.Generate.Run =
      Darklang.Generate.Json.fromSample "Order" (Darklang.Generate.input "7f3a1c9e...")

That fn is the whole recipe. A file argument was stored once as a content-addressed
blob and is named by hash, so the saved fn is pure: same body, same output, on any
machine that has the blob. `view` shows it, `edit` edits it, `deps` lists what it
reads, `status` and `commit` count it and its outputs as ordinary changes.

## Running it again

  $ dark generate Acme.Orders.orderGenerator
    ran Acme.Orders.orderGenerator into Acme.Orders:
      = Order  unchanged
      = OrderLine  unchanged
      = parse  unchanged
    nothing to do: every output already holds this content

`=` means the output hashed to what the name already holds, so no op was recorded.
That is what content addressing buys: a regenerated module only moves the names
whose content moved, and only their dependents follow.

A file on disk is not watched. To take a changed sample, run the SAME library
command again: it stores the new blob, rewrites the saved generator (`updated`),
and re-runs it. Only what changed is `~`.

An input that is a package item is a dependency like any other:

  $ dark generate Dark.mirror --type Acme.Core.Money --into Acme.Test
    wrote Acme.Test.moneyGenerator          # its body: mirror<Acme.Core.Money> ()
    ran Acme.Test.moneyGenerator into Acme.Test:
      + Money                               # same hash as Acme.Core.Money
  $ dark type Acme.Core.Money = { ... one more field ... }
    1 dependent repointed: Acme.Test.moneyGenerator
    regenerated by Acme.Test.moneyGenerator: Money

Edit the input and the saved generator repoints and re-runs in your draft as part
of propagation, and its outputs' dependents follow in turn. `propagate pin
<generator>` stops that, like any pin; `follow` catches it up and re-runs; `undo`
on the input brings the outputs back down with it. A merge or rebase repoints in
the SCM layer without re-running; the next edit, or `generate <name>`, catches up.

A document from the network is a two-step, and the generator itself stays pure:

  $ dark generate OpenApi.client --fetch https://api.x.com/openapi.json --into Acme.X
    fetched https://api.x.com/openapi.json as blob 8bc31ceb (2362 B)
    wrote Acme.X.xGenerator                 # doc comment: fetched from <url>
    ...
  $ dark generate Acme.X.xGenerator --refresh
    fetched ... as blob 8bc31ceb
    unchanged since it was last fetched

Only what the document changed moves on a refresh. The fetch goes through the
sync transport, so an API on your LAN or tailnet works; the instance policy still
has to allow the network.

## Hand edits

Editing a generated type by hand is an ordinary edit. Running the generator again
rebinds the name to the generator's output; the hand-edited version is still in
the store, and `undo <name>` brings it back. Nothing is lost, and nothing is
detected either: v1 does not record which names a generator produced.

## Effects

A generator may read the package store and nothing else. It runs at authoring
time and its output is stored, so a live read of a file or the network would make
the saved fn a lie about where the output came from; the store is the program
itself, and a generator over package inputs (a mirror, a derive) has to read it.
`generate` refuses a fn whose requirements name any other effect, and the run
executes under the guest policy, where an undeclared effect is denied at the call.
Fetch first, save the result as a file, and generate from the file.

## Writing one

A generator is a fn returning `Darklang.Generate.Run`. Build the source with
`Darklang.Generate.Source` (`recordType`, `enumType`, `fn`, `module_`,
`identifier`, `typeName`) so names are legal and layout is stable, and return
`Darklang.Generate.ofSource text`. Report a problem with
`Darklang.Generate.failed [ "why" ]`; the CLI prints the reasons and stages nothing.
Keep output deterministic (sort what has no order): same inputs, same text, same
hashes, so a re-run is a no-op.

A generator that reads a type takes it as a type argument
(`mirror<'a> ()` reads `PackageManager.Type.declarationOf<'a> ()`), which is what
makes the type a dependency of the saved fn.
"""
